WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:01.800
-Our next guest
is a best-selling

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.402
author and historian.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:05.404
His book
"Humankind: A Hopeful History"

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:06.739
is out in paperback now.

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:09.642
Please welcome to the show
Rutger Bregman, everyone.

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.644
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:17.316 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:22.989 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.790
Welcome to the show, Rutger.
-Thanks. It's great to be here.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.193
-You are our first
Dutch historian

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.361
in the history of the show.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.096
We are honored to have you.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:33.332
I believe you are
a very famous Dutch historian

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:34.534
in your home country.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:36.669
I became aware of you
when you were at --

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.271
Is it the economic forum
in Davos?

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:39.205
-Yeah, the World Economic Forum.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:40.740
-The World Economic Forum
in Davos.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.343
You were on stage in a room
full of very wealthy people.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.777
-Mm-hmm.
-They had obviously invited you.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.546
You did not charge the stage.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.281
You were an invited speaker.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.950
And I became aware of you
because you said some things

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.353
that the people in the room
did not respond very well to.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:54.921
Let's just take a quick look at
that clip.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.590
-Almost no one raises the real
issue of tax avoidance, right?

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.825
And of the rich just not paying
their fair share.

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:02.996
I mean, it feels like I'm at
a firefighters conference

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:05.098
and no one's allowed to speak
about water.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.932
This is not rocket science.

00:01:06.932 --> 00:01:08.634
I mean, we can talk for
a very long time about

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.403
all these stupid philanthropy
schemes.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.872
We can invite Bono once more,
but come on.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.008
We got to be talking
about taxes.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.842
That's it --
taxes, taxes, taxes.

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:19.212
All the rest is [bleep],
in my opinion.

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:23.615
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.615 --> 00:01:25.684
-It did not go over well.

00:01:25.684 --> 00:01:27.987
Obviously, you must have had
some expectation

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:29.656
it wouldn't go over well
when you were saying it.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.624
-You know, for some reason,
they haven't invited me back.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.792
-That was your last Davos.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.360
-I was wondering,
was it something I said?

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.329
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.731
-That happened,
and you wrote this book,

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.600
and I had an expectation
based on that.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.971
"Oh, here's a guy who points out
what's wrong,"

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.406
especially with things
such as income inequality,

00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:50.843
but you actually wrote a book
about how humankind is better

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.512
than we all give it credit for,

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:55.481
and that human beings,
at their core, are decent.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:58.651
What led you to want to write
a book about this topic?

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.986
-Well, look, this is really
a book for those who have lost

00:02:00.986 --> 00:02:02.222
their faith in humanity,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:05.191
and I think there are
quite a few of us today.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:08.961
You could summarize it
as a 400-page treatise on

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:12.165
anthropology and sociology
and philosophy and archaeology

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:14.833
in which I'm trying to
make the case

00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:18.303
that Ted Lasso is
actually right,

00:02:18.303 --> 00:02:19.872
that he's the realist.

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:21.874
He has a very realistic take on
human nature

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:24.543
and that most people, deep down,
are pretty decent.

00:02:24.543 --> 00:02:28.047
-And one of the things I thought
when I was reading it,

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:30.716
was this a hard sell
to publishers?

00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:33.219
Because it does strike me that
a lot of the books that you see

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:35.154
and a lot of the books you hear
about are about

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:36.923
what's wrong with things.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:40.226
And at the core,
you're writing optimistically

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.627
that more is right than wrong.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:42.661
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:42.661 --> 00:02:44.497
I'm a historian,
and if you study history,

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.300
it's mostly about
wars, wars, more wars,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.201
and if there's not a war, then
we call it the interbellum,

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:50.837
right,
the period in between wars.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.839
So yes, it was a hard sell.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.642
There's this really old theory
in Western culture that says

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:59.245
that our civilization is just a
thin layer, just a thin veneer,

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:02.014
and that below that lies
raw human nature,

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:05.317
that supposedly, people,
deep down, are just selfish.

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.987
And that idea comes back again
and again, you know,

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:10.189
in our philosophy,
in our culture, in our movies,

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.024
in our plays, in our novels.

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:14.960
In the book, I talk about "Lord
of the Flies," for example,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.363
this book that we were all
forced to read in high school.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.732
So not very optimistic about
human nature.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:22.535
So it's deeply embedded
in our culture.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.670
-There's a fascinating chapter
in the book where you actually

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.039
do some research and you try to
wonder what actually

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:29.008
would have happened in
a "Lord of the Flies" situation,

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:32.745
and you found, basically,
a story where this happened,

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:35.715
where some kids were shipwrecked
and the opposite happened.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.951
They became a very functional
group of kids who had to survive

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:41.620
for a great, long time
without any adult supervision.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.957
-I was just wondering whether it
had ever really happened, right?

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:47.960
Because you can write a novel,
but it would be so interesting

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.695
to find out what really happens
when you drop

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:51.331
kids on an uninhabited island.

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:53.566
It turns out that it's difficult
to do a scientific experiment

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:54.901
like that.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.869
Most parents are not
easily convinced

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:59.471
to volunteer their kids.

00:03:59.471 --> 00:04:01.440
But then I looked at

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.209
whether there's ever been
a natural experiment,

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.744
and it turned out that
it did happen in 1965.

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:07.380
There were six kids from Tonga,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.115
which is an island group
in the Pacific Ocean,

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:11.084
who were bored with school.

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:13.219
They didn't like
the school meals.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:14.854
They thought
it was pretty boring.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.856
They said, "Well, let's go on
an adventure."

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:21.093
And they "borrowed" a boat and
then ended up in a storm already

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:23.996
on the first night, drifted for
eight days, and then shipwrecked

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.732
on this island, and survived
there for 50 months.

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:30.269
And you know, if this would have
been a Hollywood movie,

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:33.172
people would say, "Oh, this is
so sentimental, so naive.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.974
This is worse than
'Love, Actually.'"

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.276
[ Laughter ]
But it really happened.

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:40.346
They survived by staying
friends, by working together.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.681
It's an example of what
some scientists call

00:04:42.681 --> 00:04:44.617
"survival of the friendliest."

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:47.486
-Obviously, you don't gloss over
the fact that there have been --

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:49.588
our history's full of
atrocities,

00:04:49.588 --> 00:04:51.523
from war to other things.

00:04:51.523 --> 00:04:55.527
And how do you reconcile that
with your core thesis?

00:04:55.527 --> 00:04:57.930
-It's one of the ironies,
obviously, if you write a book

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.333
about human decency, is that you
have to go on for

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:03.035
hundreds of pages about all the
dark chapters in human history.

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:06.472
Obviously, there's no simple,
single explanation.

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:10.509
What I do try to point out
is that the joker,

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:12.277
it does exist, right?

00:05:12.277 --> 00:05:14.347
Those kind of people do exist,
but they're very rare,

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:16.982
the kind of people who just
enjoy being sadistic

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:18.718
and enjoy hurting other people.

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:20.986
But they're a really
small minority.

00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:23.456
Most people who are on the wrong
side of history actually believe

00:05:23.456 --> 00:05:25.124
they're doing the right thing,
you know?

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:28.127
Very often, we can do terrible
things in the name of loyalty,

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.796
in the name of friendship, in
the name of comradeship, right?

00:05:30.796 --> 00:05:34.333
And that is the dark truth
about us, right?

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:36.802
Sometimes I wonder, how will
the historians of the future

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:38.003
look back on us, right?

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:40.039
Because we may be doing some
things today

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:43.075
that are really bad,
just as we can look back on,

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:45.311
say, the Middle Ages
and think torture was bad,

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:46.913
slavery was really bad,
et cetera.

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:49.549
But maybe today, we're doing
some really bad stuff as well.

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.818
-Well, I hope I'm not around
for that reckoning.

00:05:51.818 --> 00:05:55.655
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.890
I want to tie back to what
we said in the beginning.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.993
Obviously, the people
in the audience at Davos

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:02.428
is a perfect example.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.996
They were there to talk about

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:05.464
how to make the world
a better place.

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:07.232
-Mm-hmm.

00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:11.103
-And yet they reacted negatively
to the idea that

00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:14.073
paying your fair share is
probably the quickest way

00:06:14.073 --> 00:06:16.209
to solve a lot of problems.
-Mm-hmm.

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:21.413
-Were you surprised that they
didn't see that disconnect?

00:06:21.413 --> 00:06:24.149
-Well, look, the short summary
of the book would be

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:26.752
most people are pretty decent,
but power corrupts.

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:29.021
Power is this incredibly
dangerous drug

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:32.057
that often makes you
less empathetic.

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:35.228
A lot of people think that
the world is ruled by selfish,

00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:39.198
evil global elites or whatever,
but if you actually go to Davos

00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:40.799
and you meet these people,

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:43.602
you realize that the truth
is much more disturbing.

00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:46.438
You go there and they turn out
to be pretty friendly, right?

00:06:46.438 --> 00:06:48.007
They just don't see the issue.

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:50.042
They arrive in their private
jets and then watch

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:52.911
a David Attenborough movie about
how we're wrecking the planet

00:06:52.911 --> 00:06:54.280
and they're like,
"Ooh, this is really bad,"

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.082
and then they go back
in their private jet.

00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:59.018
So I think that's much more
disturbing because if it would

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:01.019
be a conspiracy,
you can just roll it up,

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:02.688
but this goes much deeper.

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:06.259
-So, I feel like I've ended on
a negative point,

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.093
and I really don't want to do
that because the book put me

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:10.763
in such a good mood
and made me so optimistic.

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:13.699
So I want to give you one last
chance to say something nice.

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:14.967
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, I will.

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:17.536
-I feel like I cornered you
into a really bad place.

00:07:17.536 --> 00:07:19.238
-It often happens.
-Yeah.

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:21.574
-What you assume in other people
is what you get out of them.

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:24.076
So if we assume that most people
are just selfish,

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:25.978
then that's the kind of world
we're going to create.

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.147
If we turn it around,
I think we can move to

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:29.915
a radically different
kind of world.

00:07:29.915 --> 00:07:32.518
If you assume that most kids are
naturally curious and creative,

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.687
we can build very different
kind of schools.

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.154
I've got one chapter in the book

00:07:36.154 --> 00:07:38.390
about the criminal justice
system in Norway.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:39.758
It's pretty astounding.

00:07:39.758 --> 00:07:42.828
So, these criminals have
the freedom to socialize

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:45.864
with the guards,
to watch movies,

00:07:45.864 --> 00:07:47.299
to build a bit of an education.

00:07:47.299 --> 00:07:48.400
They've got their own band,
actually.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:49.534
They've got their own
music label,

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:50.903
which is called
Criminal Records.

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:52.705
-There you go.
-In almost every single way,

00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:54.172
it doesn't look like a prison.

00:07:54.172 --> 00:07:55.975
But then you look at
the recidivism rate,

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:58.510
the chance that someone will
commit another crime

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.045
once he or she gets
out of prison.

00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:02.414
And it's nowhere as low
as in Norway.

00:08:02.414 --> 00:08:04.316
So it is counterintuitive.

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:07.385
It sounds a little bit, I don't
know -- How do you say that?

00:08:07.385 --> 00:08:10.856
Naive, but if you look at
the facts behind it, it works.

00:08:10.856 --> 00:08:13.092
-Alright. Well, there we go.

00:08:13.092 --> 00:08:14.960
I think that's a very nice way
to leave this,

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.862
which is we should all look for
the best in other people,

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:17.963
and maybe they'll deliver on it.

00:08:17.963 --> 00:08:19.031
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:08:19.031 --> 00:08:20.132
I really appreciate it.

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:21.600
You guys, that's Rutger Bregman.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.236
"Humankind: A Hopeful History"
is really wonderful.

00:08:24.236 --> 00:08:25.371
It's out in paperback now.

